<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the bundle's generated Info.plist. Tauri picks this file up automatically because it
  sits next to `tauri.conf.json` — see `tauri-build`'s codegen, which watches `<config dir>/Info.plist`.

  NSPrefersDisplaySafeAreaLayoutGuide = false
  ------------------------------------------
  Lets a fullscreen window use the *whole* display on a Mac with a camera housing, instead of being
  laid out below it.

  Anything built against the macOS 12+ SDK defaults this to true, which keeps windows inside the
  "safe area": on a notched MacBook that means fullscreen stops at the bottom edge of the notch and
  leaves a black band across the top — the app looks like it is refusing to fill the screen, which
  is exactly how it was reported on an M4 Air. Windows never had the equivalent because it has no
  such band.

  Safe for this app specifically, and worth checking again if the title bar ever changes: the notch
  sits in the *middle* of the top edge, and CodeFlow's title bar puts its controls at the two ends —
  window buttons and navigation on the left, AI actions on the right — with nothing between them.
  Only the bar's own gradient passes under the housing.

  NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription
  ------------------------------
  The sentence macOS shows in the "allow local network access" prompt. Since macOS 15, the first
  time this app opens a socket to a LAN address — an SSH host at 192.168.x.x, a VNC screen, an SMB
  share — the system asks, and every such connection fails until the user says yes. Without this key
  the prompt is an unexplained demand from an app the user cannot see a reason for, and a dismissed
  prompt reads afterwards exactly like a broken client: connections that time out while the same
  address answers `nc` from a terminal.

  Windows has no equivalent, which is why a host that works from the Windows build can be
  unreachable from this one on the same network.

  NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription
  ------------------------------
  «Reuniones» records what the computer plays (the other side of a Teams or Meet call) through a
  Core Audio process tap (macOS 14.6+). macOS asks for "System Audio Recording" the first time, and
  shows this sentence; without the key the tap delivers silence.

  NSMicrophoneUsageDescription
  ----------------------------
  «Dictar» and «Reuniones» read the microphone natively (cpal, AVCaptureDevice). Without this key
  macOS does not ask: the capture is refused outright, and a bundled app that touches the microphone
  with no stated reason can be terminated by the system. The key is only the sentence — the signed
  bundle must also claim `com.apple.security.device.audio-input` (Entitlements.plist), or the
  hardened runtime refuses without asking and CodeFlow never appears in Privacidad › Micrófono. Only
  asked the first time someone presses the microphone, which appears only after dictation has been
  installed from Settings. Windows has no prompt of its own; WASAPI's access-denied is reported.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>NSPrefersDisplaySafeAreaLayoutGuide</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>CodeFlow uses the microphone while you dictate into an AI field, and while you record a meeting into a note. The audio is transcribed on this computer.</string>
    <key>NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription</key>
    <string>CodeFlow records the computer's audio only while you record a meeting into a note, so the other participants are transcribed too.</string>
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>CodeFlow needs this to reach the machines on your network — SSH hosts, screens and file shares you connect to by local address.</string>
  </dict>
</plist>
